---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How we verify deals & calculate price history">
  <article class="prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">HISTORY BEFORE HYPE</div>
    <h1>What makes a deal a deal?</h1>
    <p>
      A lower price is useful only when the comparison is meaningful. DealRadar records observations
      from authorized sources and compares the same listing, model, variant, condition, and
      currency.
    </p>
    <h2>Our historical baseline</h2>
    <p>
      The default baseline is the median of prior total prices in the last 30 days. The current
      observation is excluded. Shipping must be known. We require at least eight distinct observed
      days, a span of at least seven days, and no gap greater than 48 hours. A retailer's suggested
      or crossed-out original price does not enter this calculation.
    </p>
    <h2>How savings are verified</h2>
    <p>
      Dollar savings equal the historical median minus the current item price and shipping.
      Percentage savings are that difference divided by the baseline. The conservative default
      requires at least a 15% reduction and $15 savings, current availability, a fresh price check,
      sufficient history, and no recent repeat publication. Taxes are excluded because they depend
      on the buyer's location.
    </p>
    <h2>A score, not a product rating</h2>
    <p>
      Scores from 0–100 combine percentage savings, dollar savings, evidence strength, freshness,
      availability, and seller confidence when supplied. Initial weights are 30, 15, 25, 15, 10, and
      5 respectively. Scores describe the strength of a price opportunity. They are not product
      reviews. Administrators can change these weights and category adjustments.
    </p>
    <h2>Observed lows have limits</h2>
    <p>
      “Lowest observed” describes our records, never a guarantee of the lowest price anywhere. A
      30-day low requires history reaching at least 30 days back, at least 30 distinct observed days
      in the relevant period, and sufficiently continuous records. Sparse history cannot establish
      this claim.
    </p>
    <h2>Evaluate the whole purchase</h2>
    <p>
      Check the exact model, capacity, condition, seller, warranty, delivery cost, and return
      policy. A strong discount does not make a product right for you. Confirm the final price with
      the retailer; offers can change between checks.
    </p>
    <h2>Demonstration data</h2>
    <p>
      Demo mode uses fictional products and simulated prices and history to make the platform
      testable without credentials. Every page is labeled, blocked from indexing, and prevented from
      publishing to real social accounts.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/deals">Explore the deal feed →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
